  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1110 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA potent upper level trough will move onto the West Coast on\nSunday, nudging the upper ridging over our area east. As the \nridge starts to slide east, southwesterly flow will bring \nincreased moisture at mid and upper levels from the Pacific. The \nhigher moisture in the mountains and some weak upslope from \nsoutheasterly surface winds could produce some isolated to \nscattered showers in the higher terrain and areas generally north \nof I-76 tomorrow afternoon. While most of this activity is \nexpected to remain on the weaker side, we could see an isolated \nstorm or two. MLCAPE will be weak, but looking at lapse rates and\nDCAPEs, any storm that can get going could have the potential for\nsome gusty winds (maybe around 40kts). The showers and potential storms\nare forecast to move northeast into the late evening, but no \nimpacts are expected.\n\nThe upper trough is forecast to dive into southern California on \nMonday, progressing into Arizona on Tuesday. As the trough moves \neast/southeast, moisture will continue to flow into the area, \nincreasing PWs to around 200% of normal. A few weak disturbances \nwill likely move through the southwest flow at mid levels, \nproviding weak lift for showers and storms Monday and Tuesday. \nWidespread coverage is not anticipated with this activity, with \nthe best chances for precip over the mountains. While coverage \nwill be minimal, we will want to keep an eye on the localized \nheavy rain potential both days due to the anomalous moisture. A \ncold front is still poised to move through the forecast are late \nMonday into early Tuesday. Overall impacts from the front will be \npretty limited, other than slightly cooler temperatures for \nTuesday and Wednesday. Snow chances for the mountains continue to \ndecrease as each new model run comes in. While there will likely \nbe a few rounds of flurries and a dusting at the highest \nelevations, temperatures just look too warm to support decent snow\ntotals (snow levels are generally 12,000+ft for Monday through \nWednesday and SLRs are below 5). \n\nModels now have better agreement that the strong upper low will \neventually move into southeast Colorado by Thursday. The main \ndifference between models now is where they develop the surface\nlow, impacting where the heaviest rain band forms. If the surface\nlow ends up a bit closer to our area, we could see some wrap \naround moisture, bringing heavy rain potential for Wednesday and \nThursday. However, if the surface low ends up too far south and \neast, our area will end up on the drier side of things. The exact \nlocation of the axis of heavier precip will be very difficult to \nforecast, and very impactful. Models are hinting at some pretty \nsubstantial rain amounts with this system, but right now, the \nmajority of ensemble members keep the highest rain totals to the \nsouth and east of our forecast area. The best chance for heavier \nrain totals for our area will be in Lincoln County. All this being\nsaid, even if we don't end up with the widespread heavy rainfall,\nmoisture will be high enough that localized flash flooding will \nbe a concern for any storms that pop up.\n\nThis system and the better rain chances will start to push off to\nthe east on Friday. The speed at which that system exits and the \nnext moves in is still up in the air. The GFS wants to bring in \nthe next shortwave and frontal system almost immediately over the \nweekend, however the Euro wants to delay the second system and \nhold onto the first for a bit longer. Either way, the weather with\nthe highest potential impacts over the next week will be that \nWednesday through Thursday time period.",
